    anatomy: [
      'Paired ovoid nuclear masses either side of the third ventricle, divided by the internal medullary lamina into <b>anterior</b>, <b>medial</b> and <b>lateral</b> groups, with intralaminar and midline nuclei inside the lamina and the <b>reticular nucleus</b> as a GABAergic shell.',
      'Functional classes: <b>first-order relay</b> (LGN, MGN, VPL/VPM, VA/VL), <b>higher-order/association</b> (MD, pulvinar, LP), <b>limbic</b> (anterior, LD), <b>non-specific</b> (intralaminar CM/Pf, midline).',
      'The reticular nucleus receives corticothalamic collaterals and inhibits relay cells — the architecture of thalamic attentional gating and of sleep spindles.'
    ],
    cyto: ['Relay (thalamocortical) glutamatergic neurons plus local GABAergic interneurons (sparse in rodents, plentiful in primates); reticular nucleus is entirely GABAergic.'],
    circuit: [
      { dir: 'Afferent', txt: 'Sensory pathways (optic tract→LGN, auditory→MGN, medial lemniscus/spinothalamic→VPL), cerebellum (dentate→VL), basal ganglia (GPi/SNr→VA/VL, ventral pallidum→MD), mammillary bodies→anterior nucleus via mammillothalamic tract, plus massive corticothalamic feedback.' },
      { dir: 'Efferent', txt: 'Topographic projections to all cortex via the internal capsule (anterior, superior and posterior thalamic radiations); MD→prefrontal, anterior→cingulate, pulvinar→parietal/temporal.' },
      { dir: 'Loop', txt: 'Thalamocortical resonance at 8–12 Hz (alpha) and spindle frequencies; the thalamus is the obligatory node of every CSTC loop and of the ascending arousal system.' }
    ],
    fn: [
      '<b>Gating and gain control</b> of cortical input: burst vs tonic firing modes change the message from "something changed" to "here is the detail".',
      '<b>MD nucleus:</b> sustains prefrontal rule representations — inactivation impairs working memory without impairing sensation.',
      '<b>Anterior nuclei:</b> obligatory for episodic memory (the diencephalic arm of the extended hippocampal system).',
      '<b>Pulvinar:</b> attentional filtering and distractor suppression; <b>intralaminar nuclei:</b> arousal and level of consciousness.'
    ],
    chem: ['Cholinergic (brainstem PPT/LDT) and noradrenergic input switches relay cells from burst to tonic mode at wake onset; T-type calcium channels generate spindles and the 3 Hz spike-wave of absence epilepsy (ethosuximide’s target).'],
